    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hypoxia, or low oxygen tension, is a common feature of the tumor\r\nmicroenvironment that is linked to tumor resistance to radiation therapy, and thus, is also\r\nassociated with poor patient prognosis. The complement system, a set of serum proteins\r\nwhich has traditionally been understood as part of the immune system, has also been\r\nfound to be tumor-promoting when upregulated. However, the interaction between\r\nhypoxia and the complement system is not fully understood. In patients with colorectal\r\ncancer, an increased level of complement component C5a receptor (C5aR1) expression is\r\nstrongly correlated with poor patient prognosis, and we have found that C5aR1\r\nexpression is also increased in hypoxia. We predicted that the hypoxia inducible factors\r\n(HIFs) may be playing a role in the transcriptional upregulation of C5aR1. We assessed\r\nat what level the induction was occurring at—transcriptional or translational—and\r\nproduced complicating data. In a subset of experiments, hypoxic induction was observed\r\nwith dependence on HIF-1α, which was predicted to bind upstream of the C5AR1\r\npromoter. We also observed that induction was lost in some cells and absent in different\r\ncell lines. We conclude that C5aR1 may be induced in hypoxia but also depends on other\r\ncellular factors. The results of this study provide novel insight into how the upregulation\r\nof C5aR1 occurs in colorectal cancer. Given the relationship between C5aR1 levels and\r\npatient outcome, understanding how receptor expression is regulated may allow for the\r\ndevelopment of new therapeutic approaches to treating colorectal cancer.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
